calc.py
--------------
def add()
    return "addition"

def sub():
    return "subtraction"
if __name__ == "__main__":  # inorder to avoide all the function or satements to be called this is used
    print(add())
    print(sub())
print(__name__)

demo1.py
------
import calc

result = calc.add()
print("result:", result)
print(__name__)

if __name__ == "__main__": ensures that the statements inside the if block execute only when the Python file is run directly, not when it is imported into another file.

When imported, __name__ contains the module name (e.g., "calc"), so the condition becomes False.
